---
import PageHeader from '../components/ui/PageHeader.astro';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { PAGES } from '../lib/seo';
import { REPOS } from '../lib/site';

const updated = 'November 13, 2026';
---

<BaseLayout {...PAGES['/privacy']}>
  <PageHeader
    eyebrow="Legal"
    title="Privacy policy"
    lede="What Shetu collects, why, and what it never sees. The short version: no tracking on this site, no telemetry in the app, and your secrets are encrypted before they reach the sync server."
  />

  <section class="mx-auto max-w-[760px] px-5 py-16 sm:px-6 md:py-20">
    <article class="docs-prose">
      <p class="text-sm text-muted">Last updated: {updated}</p>

      <h2>Who this policy covers</h2>
      <p>
        Shetu is run by the Shetu maintainers ("we"). This policy covers this
        website, the Shetu desktop app, and the Shetu sync server that release
        builds of the app sign in to.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you or your organization run your own Shetu server, whoever runs
        that server decides what happens to the data on it. This policy does
        not cover servers we do not operate.
      </p>

      <h2>This website</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>No cookies, no analytics and no third-party tracking scripts. Fonts are served from this site.</li>
        <li>The site is hosted on Cloudflare, which processes standard request data such as your IP address to deliver pages and block abuse.</li>
        <li>Download buttons point to GitHub Releases. GitHub's own privacy statement applies there.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>The desktop app</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>The app sends no telemetry, analytics or crash reports.</li>
        <li>SSH, SFTP and port forwarding connect straight from your device to your hosts. That traffic never passes through our servers.</li>
        <li>Your local database is encrypted on your device. Sign-in secrets are kept in your operating system's keychain.</li>
        <li>The app checks GitHub for updates, which shares your IP address and app version with GitHub.</li>
        <li>To show profile pictures, the app loads images from <a href="https://gravatar.com">Gravatar</a> using a SHA-256 hash of your email address and of your teammates' email addresses. Gravatar receives that hash and your IP address. If you signed in with Google or GitHub, your picture loads from Google or GitHub instead. Without a picture, the app shows your initials.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What the sync server stores</h2>
      <p>To create your account and sync your vault, the server stores:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Account details:</strong> your email address, your name, how you sign in (password, Google or GitHub), and, if you sign in with Google or GitHub, your account ID and profile picture link from them.</li>
        <li><strong>Sign-in material:</strong> a password verifier and salts derived on your device, never your password itself, plus your public key and a hash used for account recovery.</li>
        <li><strong>Activity dates:</strong> when you created your account, verified your email and last signed in.</li>
        <li><strong>Devices:</strong> a random ID for each device you sign in on, the app's user agent, and hashed refresh tokens.</li>
        <li><strong>Your vault:</strong> encrypted records. A few fields stay readable so sync can work, such as record names, tags and timestamps. Host addresses, usernames, passwords, private keys and commands are encrypted on your device. See <a href="/security">Security</a> for the full list.</li>
        <li><strong>Teams:</strong> the teams you belong to, your role, and invites, including the invited person's email address.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The server also keeps request logs (IP address, time, request path and
        result) to run and secure the service, and holds IP addresses in memory
        for up to a minute to apply rate limits.
      </p>
      <p>
        If the server requires email verification, we send a one-time code to
        your email address through an email delivery provider.
      </p>

      <h2>Signing in with Google or GitHub</h2>
      <p>
        Signing in with Google is optional. If you choose it, Shetu asks Google
        only for the basic <code>openid</code>, <code>email</code> and
        <code>profile</code> permissions. From your Google account we receive:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>your email address, which becomes your Shetu account email;</li>
        <li>your name, shown in the app and to members of teams you join;</li>
        <li>your Google account ID, used to recognize you the next time you sign in;</li>
        <li>your profile picture link, stored to show your picture in the app and to members of teams you join, and updated each time you sign in with Google.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        We use this data only to create your Shetu account and sign you in.
        The Google access token is used once during sign-in and is not stored.
        We do not access your Gmail, Drive, contacts or any other Google data,
        do not sell or share Google user data, do not use it for advertising,
        and do not use it to train AI models. Shetu's use of information
        received from Google APIs adheres to the
        <a href="https://developers.google.com/terms/api-services-user-data-policy">Google API Services User Data Policy</a>,
        including the Limited Use requirements.
      </p>
      <p>
        GitHub sign-in works the same way: Shetu asks only for the
        <code>user:email</code> permission and stores your email, name,
        profile picture link and GitHub account ID.
      </p>
      <p>
        You can remove Shetu's access at any time from your
        <a href="https://myaccount.google.com/connections">Google account settings</a>
        or your GitHub application settings. Deleting your Shetu account, as
        described below, deletes the Google or GitHub data we stored.
      </p>

      <h2>How we use it</h2>
      <p>
        Only to provide sign-in, sync and team sharing, to keep the service
        secure, and to stop abuse. We do not sell your data, show ads, or
        share your data with anyone except:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>providers that host the service or deliver its email, only as needed to do that;</li>
        <li>other members of a team you join, who can see your name, email and the vaults you share with them;</li>
        <li>authorities, when the law requires it.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        We cannot read your encrypted vault, so we cannot hand it over in
        readable form to anyone.
      </p>

      <h2>How long we keep it</h2>
      <p>
        We keep your account data for as long as your account exists. When you
        delete a record, the server keeps a small deletion marker so your other
        devices learn about the change. Expired refresh tokens and request logs
        are not kept longer than needed to run and secure the service.
      </p>

      <h2>Your choices and rights</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>You can edit or delete your hosts, keys, snippets and other records in the app at any time.</li>
        <li>You can ask us to delete your account and everything stored with it, or for a copy of the account details we hold.</li>
        <li>Depending on where you live, you may have further rights, such as to correct your data or object to how we use it. Ask and we will help.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The app does not have a self-service account deletion button yet. To
        make a request, <a href={`${REPOS.monorepo}/issues/new`}>open an issue on GitHub</a>
        titled "Privacy request". Do not put your email address or other
        personal details in the issue, since issues are public. We will reply
        there to confirm your identity privately.
      </p>

      <h2>Security</h2>
      <p>
        Your vault is encrypted on your device with a key derived from your
        password. If you lose both your password and your recovery kit, nobody
        can decrypt your vault, including us. Read <a href="/security">how Shetu protects your data</a>
        and report vulnerabilities through
        <a href={`${REPOS.monorepo}/security/advisories/new`}>GitHub Security Advisories</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Children</h2>
      <p>
        Shetu is a tool for developers and system administrators. It is not
        meant for children under 13, and we do not knowingly collect their data.
      </p>

      <h2>Where data is processed</h2>
      <p>
        Our service providers may process data in countries other than yours.
        Your vault stays encrypted wherever it is stored.
      </p>

      <h2>Changes</h2>
      <p>
        We will update this page when our practices change and change the date
        at the top. The full history of this page is public in the
        <a href={REPOS.web}>shetu-web repository</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Contact</h2>
      <p>
        Questions about this policy go to
        <a href={`${REPOS.monorepo}/issues`}>GitHub Issues</a>. Please keep
        personal details out of public issues.
      </p>
    </article>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
